### Why start simple and measure?

Premature scaling wastes effort and adds complexity you may never need, while under-scaling causes outages. Every optimization just moves the bottleneck somewhere else, so there is no point scaling a tier that is not the current constraint. You measure, relieve the actual constraint, and the bottleneck shifts to the next tier.

```java
package com.bank.monitoring;

import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.Timer;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class BottleneckDetector {

    private final Timer dbQueryTimer;
    private final Timer cacheTimer;
    private final Timer externalCallTimer;

    public BottleneckDetector(MeterRegistry registry) {
        this.dbQueryTimer = Timer.builder("bottleneck.db.query")
            .description("Database query latency")
            .register(registry);
        this.cacheTimer = Timer.builder("bottleneck.cache.lookup")
            .description("Cache lookup latency")
            .register(registry);
        this.externalCallTimer = Timer.builder("bottleneck.external.call")
            .description("External service call latency")
            .register(registry);
    }
}
```

```text
The iterative loop:
  1. Measure under real load
  2. Find the single current bottleneck
  3. Relieve exactly that bottleneck
  4. The bottleneck shifts to the next tier
  5. Repeat
```

### Stage 1: How do indexes and query optimization help?

At one user to a few thousand, the simplest design is a single application server talking to a single database. The first bottleneck is almost always slow database queries. Adding the right indexes turns a balance lookup from a full table scan into an instant index seek.

```sql
-- BEFORE: full table scan (slow as data grows)
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001';
-- Execution plan: Seq Scan on accounts  cost=0.00..1250.00

-- FIX: add an index
CREATE INDEX idx_accounts_account_id ON accounts(account_id);

-- AFTER: index seek (fast regardless of table size)
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001';
-- Execution plan: Index Scan using idx_accounts_account_id  cost=0.00..8.27
```

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Query;
import org.springframework.data.repository.CrudRepository;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface AccountRepository extends CrudRepository<Account, Long> {

    // WRONG: N+1 query pattern
    // List<Account> findAll(); // then loop and call getTransactions()

    // CORRECT: fetch join to avoid N+1
    @Query("SELECT a FROM Account a LEFT JOIN FETCH a.recentTransactions " +
           "WHERE a.accountId = :accountId")
    Account findWithTransactions(String accountId);
}
```

```text
Impact of indexing:
  Before: balance lookup 120ms (full scan of 1M rows)
  After:  balance lookup 2ms (index seek)
  Cost:   zero infrastructure change, just one SQL statement
```

### Stage 2: How do you make the app stateless?

As traffic grows, the single application server becomes the bottleneck. Make the app stateless by moving session state out of instance memory into a shared store, then run several instances behind a load balancer.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.session.data.redis.config.annotation.web.http.EnableRedisHttpSession;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
@EnableRedisHttpSession
public class StatelessConfig {
    // Sessions stored in Redis, not in JVM heap
    // Any instance can handle any request
}
```

```yaml
# Nginx load balancer config
upstream account-service {
    server 192.168.1.10:8080;
    server 192.168.1.11:8080;
    server 192.168.1.12:8080;
}

server {
    listen 80;
    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://account-service;
    }
}
```

```text
Before: 1 instance handling 500 req/s (maxed out)
After:  3 instances behind LB -> 1500 req/s capacity
```

### Stage 3: How do caching and read replicas reduce database load?

The database becomes the bottleneck again under read-heavy load. Three fixes apply together: caching hot data in Redis, adding read replicas, and putting a CDN in front of static assets.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.cache.annotation.Cacheable;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class BalanceService {

    private final AccountRepository repository;

    public BalanceService(AccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Cacheable(value = "balances", key = "#accountId")
    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        return repository.findBalanceByAccountId(accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
Read scaling strategy:
  Cache (Redis):     95% of reads served from memory (microseconds)
  Read replicas:     5% cache misses go to replicas, not primary
  CDN:               static assets served from edge (zero server load)
  Primary DB:        handles only writes and rare cache misses
```

### Stage 4: How do async queues and rate limiting smooth spikes?

Write throughput and slow synchronous chains become the bottleneck. A service does its critical step and publishes an event to Kafka, letting downstream work happen later and letting the queue absorb spikes.

```java
package com.bank.transfer;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class AsyncTransferService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, TransferEvent> kafka;

    public AsyncTransferService(AccountRepository accountRepo,
                                 KafkaTemplate<String, TransferEvent> kafka) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
        this.kafka = kafka;
    }

    @Transactional
    public String transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        accountRepo.debit(from, amount);
        accountRepo.credit(to, amount);
        // Async: statement, notification, analytics happen later
        kafka.send("transfer-events",
            new TransferEvent(from, to, amount));
        return "Transfer completed";
    }
}
```

```text
Queue as shock absorber:
  Spike: 10,000 transfers/s burst
  Producer: writes to Kafka at burst rate
  Consumer: drains at steady 2,000/s
  Queue depth: absorbs the 8,000/s difference
  Result: no dropped requests, statements catch up in minutes
```

### Stage 5: How do microservices and sharding provide independent scaling?

As the codebase and team grow, the monolith becomes the bottleneck. Split into microservices so each part scales, deploys, and fails independently. Shard the database by account ID so write capacity grows beyond a single primary.

```text
Monolith -> Microservices:
  account-service  (PostgreSQL, sharded by account_id)
  transfer-service (PostgreSQL, references account-service API)
  statement-service (Cassandra, high write volume)
  notification-service (Redis, ephemeral)
```

```java
package com.bank.shard;

public class ShardRouter {

    private static final int SHARD_COUNT = 4;

    public int getShard(String accountId) {
        // Consistent hash: same account always goes to same shard
        return Math.abs(accountId.hashCode()) % SHARD_COUNT;
    }
}
```

```text
Sharding by account_id:
  Shard 0: accounts A000-A249  (DB node 1)
  Shard 1: accounts A250-A499  (DB node 2)
  Shard 2: accounts A500-A749  (DB node 3)
  Shard 3: accounts A750-A999  (DB node 4)
  Write capacity: 4x single primary
```

### Stage 6: How do auto-scaling and multi-region complete the picture?

At the million-user scale, manual scaling and single-region deployment become the bottleneck. Auto-scaling matches capacity to demand automatically, and multi-region puts the system in multiple geographic regions for lower latency and resilience.

```yaml
# Kubernetes HPA for auto-scaling
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: transfer-service-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: transfer-service
  minReplicas: 3
  maxReplicas: 50
  metrics:
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: cpu
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 70
```

```text
Multi-region deployment:
  Region 1 (Mumbai):   Primary for Indian customers
  Region 2 (Singapore): Replica for Southeast Asia
  Global LB:           Routes to nearest healthy region
  Failover:            If Mumbai is down, Singapore serves all traffic
```

### What is the complete evolution summary?

| Stage | Users | Bottleneck | Fix | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 1-1K | Slow queries | Indexes | Zero |
| 2 | 1K-10K | Single server | Stateless + LB | Low |
| 3 | 10K-100K | DB reads | Cache + replicas + CDN | Medium |
| 4 | 100K-500K | Sync chains | Async queues + rate limit | Medium |
| 5 | 500K-1M | Monolith | Microservices + sharding | High |
| 6 | 1M+ | Manual ops | Auto-scale + multi-region | High |
